This is Hidden Brain. I'm Shankar Vedantam. In October 1967, the world's greatest chess minds gathered in Seuss, Tunisia, to face off at the interzonal tournament, the high-stakes gateway to the World Chess Championship. The early games were dominated by the American prodigy, Bobby Fisher. He was a 24-year-old grandmaster with an IQ rumor to be at the genius level of 180. He was the favorite to win it all. But despite his overwhelming lead, the tournament quickly spiraled into a masterclass in self-sabotage. Bobby Fisher issued a series of escalating demands regarding his playing schedule.
这里是隐藏的大脑。我是尚卡尔·韦丹塔姆。1967年10月，世界顶尖的国际象棋大师们聚集在突尼斯苏斯，参加洲际锦标赛，这是通往世界国际象棋锦标赛的高风险关口。比赛早期由美国天才少年鲍比·费舍尔主导。他是一位24岁的特级大师，据传智商高达180的天才水平。他是赢得一切的热门人选。但尽管他占据压倒性优势，锦标赛很快就演变成了一场自我破坏的大师课。鲍比·费舍尔对他的比赛赛程提出了一系列升级的要求。

2
00:00:48.000 --> 00:01:28.000
When organizers refused to rearrange the event to suit him, he walked out, withdrawing from the tournament while in first place, and throwing away years of work in a single fit of frustration. The walkout was a window into a profound emotional ineptness that lived right alongside Bobby Fisher's brilliance. Throughout his career, he operated in a state of constant relational friction, alienating the very coaches and friends who try to support him. At his chess matches, he frequently violated the social norms of the game, arriving late and refusing to make eye contact with the player sitting across from him.
当组织者拒绝重新安排赛事来迎合他时，他一走了之，在处于第一名的时候退出了比赛，在一时沮丧中扔掉了多年的心血。这次罢赛让我们得以一窥鲍比·费舍尔辉煌背后的巨大情感无能。在他的整个职业生涯中，他一直处于持续的人际摩擦状态中，疏远了那些试图支持他的教练和朋友。在他的国际象棋比赛中，他经常违反这项运动的社交规范，迟到并拒绝与坐在对面的选手进行眼神交流。

