00:01:28.000 --> 00:02:11.000
Bobby Fisher possessed a mind that could see a dozen moves ahead on a chess board, but he struggled with the basic moves of social and emotional life. This week on Hidden Brain and in a companion episode on Hidden Brain Plus, the logic of emotion and how feelings make us smarter. Think back to the last time you made a decision you regret it. Perhaps you felt blinded by rage or paralyzed by fear. In those moments, it feels like our emotions are obstacles to our success, a vestige of our primitive brains getting in a moment.
鲍比·费舍尔拥有一颗能在棋盘上预见十多步的大脑，但他却在社交和情感生活的基本动作上举步维艰。本周的“隐藏大脑”以及“隐藏大脑Plus”的配套节目将探讨情感逻辑以及情感如何让我们变得更聪明。回想一下你上次做出后悔决定的情景。也许你曾觉得被愤怒蒙蔽双眼，或是被恐惧吓得瘫痪。在那些时刻，我们感觉自己的情感是成功的阻碍，是我们原始大脑残余的影响。

4
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:37.000
in the way of our rational minds. But research in recent decades into the world of emotions suggests that this idea is profoundly wrong. Emotions are central to our ability to understand, attend, and to prioritize. They aren't obstacles that block our path. They are food and fuel for the journey. Peter Salovey is a psychologist at Yale University.
阻碍了我们的理性思维。但近几十年来对情感世界的研究所表明，这种观点是大错特错的。情感对于我们理解、关注和确定优先级的核心能力至关重要。它们不是阻挡我们道路的障碍。它们是这段旅程的养分和燃料。彼得·萨洛维是耶鲁大学的心理学家。

